# Rejected / superseded approaches

## C4-only finite-q transfer bound

The first uniform finite-q attempt used only a periodic `C^4` trapezoid error
bound. It was mathematically sound as a sufficient-bound strategy but far too
loose: the common Krawczyk box did not certify below q=2000, and a later scan
placed its sufficient threshold near q=43160.

It was superseded by the analytic-strip Fourier alias bound, which uses the
same model and same Krawczyk box but exploits analyticity. The improved
certificate gives the sufficient uniform threshold `even q >= 190`.

The old script is retained under `rejected/` for auditability and is not part
of the clean replay.
